   121	### 1.4 The verdict rule (`verdict-rule.json`, hashed; `score_comparison.py` with a test and a mutant per rule)
   122	
   123	1. **The cutoff:** one for every matter, the end of the invocation's last day (09-20 in the full run). Libraries are read as they stand then.
   124	   - Closed questions still count.
   125	   - Timeliness is not scored. The days from first source to opening are printed beside.
   126	2. **Covers (mapping, A2's form, blind):**
   127	   - **The rule:** both families mark the question `opened`, or `narrower` with both saying it carries the matter's main outcome
   128	     (ACCEPTANCE-DESIGN A; `tally_week.covered`). `broader` and `missed` do not cover.
   129	   - **Blinding:** the readers see each draw's library under a random X/Y label, the key outside the repository, and are told to ignore
   130	     style.
   131	   - **Disagreements:** two third readers, one per family. A remaining split is resolved per counting (item 7, order: covers > does not
   132	     cover).
   133	3. **Same question:**
   134	   - **A question** is an id in a system-draw's library at the cutoff, the system's own identity. Distinct questions are distinct ids.
   135	   - **Matter to question:** the mapping rule above.
   136	   - **Question to question:** two questions of one library are the same bet when the same outcome settles both and their answers cannot move
   137	     apart (A1's rule; D248). Of such a pair, **the earlier-opened question is the original and the later one the duplicate** (as
   138	     `week/brief-A2.md` named the order).
   139	   - **During mapping:** the readers, who read each whole library, also mark its duplicates.
   140	   - **Distinct bets** are the ids less the duplicates both families mark. The 30-question minimum counts distinct bets. A split duplicate is
   141	     counted both ways, and the minimum must hold both ways.
   142	   - **On cards:** a duplicate stays a question in the worth guardrail.
   143	4. **The cards and their marks:**
   144	   - **Worth tracking:** worth, trivia or duplicate. Each card lists its library's other questions in opening order, with their opening days.
   145	     `duplicate` is marked only on the later question of a same-bet pair.
   146	   - **Answer:** supported, unsupported, insufficient, or no answer (a question not judged by the cutoff).
   147	   - **Cannot tell:** a split after the debate rounds, resolved in each counting by item 7.
   148	5. **Served in a draw:** at least one covering question is marked `worth` and supported, in each counting after item 7's resolution. A matter **served by a system** is served in
   149	   at least 2 of its 3 draws.
   150	6. **The quantities:** a = served only by the new system; b = served only by the old; N = all listed matters.
   151	7. **The two countings.** A split ("cannot tell" after the debate rounds) resolves to one of the marks its readers actually gave.
   152	   - **Counting 1:** the new system's cards take the most favourable mark given; the old system's cards take the least favourable.
   153	   - **Counting 2:** the reverse.
   154	   - **The order:** for the mapping, covers > does not cover; for the answer, supported > insufficient > unsupported; for worth, worth >
   155	     duplicate or trivia.
   156	   - **How a resolved mark counts:** it counts as that mark. A resolved supported or unsupported mark enters the decided denominators. A
   157	     resolved insufficient mark stays out of them, as any insufficient mark does.
   158	   - A guardrail subgroup is **exercised** only with at least 10 cards decided **without** a split. A subgroup that is not exercised cannot
   159	     breach. It blocks only a practical lead; a loss established otherwise still stands.
   160	8. **The guardrails** (D428's measure, which D665 turns into these guardrails):
   161	   - **The sample:** the random guardrail sample only, 35 questions per system-draw by seed 20261117, or all of them when a library holds
   162	     fewer. Covering cards are not in these shares. The three draws are pooled per system.
   163	   - **The shares:**
   164	     - the share of **decided** figure-question answers (supported or unsupported) that are unsupported;
   165	     - the same for yes/no questions;
   166	     - the share of decided worth marks that are trivia or duplicate.
   167	   - **Reported beside, not in the shares:** insufficient and no answer, with the number of splits and how each counting resolved them.
   168	   - **The bar:** the new system's share may not be more than 10 points above the old system's.
   169	   - **Exercised:** each subgroup needs at least 10 cards per system decided without a split (item 7), or it is "not exercised".
   170	9. **The controls,** outside every product count:
   171	   - 6 planted wrong answers (a rung moved, 3 per system). Each needs an explicit unsupported or insufficient mark, or the reading is invalid.
   172	   - 4 supported controls, confirmed first by a separate reader.
   173	   - 3 hidden repeats.
   174	10. **No release verdict without six complete draws.** A draw that cannot finish (stopped after D449's one redo, or by its guard) gives no
   175	    release verdict. What finished goes to Alex as a diagnostic, with the cause.
   176	    - **D431's "same day" for a run longer than one day:** a new-system draw takes about 20 h, so one calendar day cannot hold it.
   177	      - The six counted draws are launched within one hour of each other and run concurrently.
   178	      - A D449 redo inside a draw counts as part of that draw.
   179	      - Every counted send falls within 72 h of the first launch.
   180	      - A breach makes the result a diagnostic.
   181	      - Recorded as decision D667 before any paid send, replacing D431's one-calendar-day reading for this run only.
   182	    - No replacement draw counts toward the verdict. The reviewers read "same day" differently (Codex: the send day; the Claude reviewer:
   183	      the window), so the council took the cheaper, reversible course (D439): a later replacement would be a diagnostic only.
   184	11. **The verdicts, tested in this order:**
   185	    1. **Invalid:** a plant without an explicit rejection, fewer than 3 of 4 supported controls accepted, or fewer than 2 of 3 repeats marked
   186	       the same.
   187	    2. **Too small:** N < 20, or fewer than 30 distinct questions in any system-draw.
   188	    3. **Practical lead:** a − b ≥ max(3, ⌈0.15·N⌉) and a ≥ 2b under both countings, with every guardrail exercised and passing under both.
   189	    4. **Practical loss:** b − a ≥ max(3, ⌈0.15·N⌉) and b ≥ 2a under both countings (needing no guardrail exercised), or a breach in an
   190	       exercised guardrail under both.
   191	    5. **No practical winner:** everything else.
   192	12. **Printed beside, not deciding:**
   193	    - D428's limits next to the guardrails;

